  url = {https://doi.org/10.1109/re68928.2026.00039},
  abstract = {In automotive software development, verifying the consistency of requirement specifications is a critical yet laborintensive task, as undetected inconsistencies can propagate into design and implementation defects. This verification relies heavily on the domain knowledge of experienced engineers-the articulable principles, rules, and analytical techniques they apply when judging consistency. We report on our experience deploying an AI agent for automated requirements consistency checking. Drawing on an established knowledge classification framework from cognitive science, we categorize the domain knowledge into three types-Situational, Conceptual, and Procedural-and, through an ablation study over 53 industrial verification items, empirically evaluate each type's contribution to agent performance. Without any domain knowledge, the agent detected 47% of the issues identified by human experts. With full domain knowledge, this coverage improved to 74%, and false alarms decreased by 42%. Our ablation revealed that Conceptual Knowledge was the primary driver of Human Review Coverage improvement (66% alone), while Procedural Knowledge most effectively reduced false alarms. Notably, most individual knowledge layers showed limited effectiveness in isolation, but the layers exhibited strong synergistic effects when combined. These results provide practical guidelines for organizations seeking to deploy AI agents for requirements engineering tasks.  © 2026 IEEE.},
