  abstract = {In software requirements engineering, ambiguous non-functional requirements can cause design inconsistencies and poor software quality. Manual ambiguity detection is time consuming and inefficient, particularly for large-scale requirements documents. Prior studies lack in handling ambiguous non-functional requirements and often analyze simple English statements instead of discussing NFRs. In this study, we emphasize detecting anaphoric, coordination, and missing-condition ambiguity in non-functional requirements. For anaphoric ambiguity detection, we applied SpanBERT due to its contextual capturing abilities. Coordination and missing-condition ambiguities were identified using a rule-based approach, i.e., spaCy's Named Entity Ruler. We conducted experiments on a significant dataset consisting of 2400 non-functional requirements from 16 different domains. Experimental results show that the suggested approach performs better than ChatGPT in terms of accuracy and consistency across the evaluated datasets. The proposed approach can assist requirement engineers and project managers in identifying ambiguities at an early stage of development. Timely detection helps reduce rework, associated costs, schedule risks, and improves overall software quality. © 2026 IEEE.},
