  abstract = {The quality and consistency of airborne software requirements are paramount for aviation safety and airworthiness certification. However, traditional manual reviews struggle to effectively perform global checks across multiple requirements-particularly in identifying terminology inconsistencies, logical anomalies, and functional redundancies scattered throughout large-scale requirement sets. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a novel requirement review framework that integrates the semantic understanding capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) with the structured reasoning capabilities of Knowledge Graphs (KGs). First, through requirement structure extraction, unstructured requirements are parsed into a graph-structured representation, leveraging variable associations to capture explicit dependencies. Subsequently, a graph-augmented consistency detection module is designed to identify anomalies and redundancies across non-adjacent requirements via topological analysis. This approach transforms the discrete requirement review process into a global, relation-aware analysis, significantly enhancing the depth of defect detection and optimizing the verification efficiency of airborne software requirements. Experimental results on a DO-178C-compliant dataset demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a Recall of 95.0%, significantly outperforming standalone LLM and retrieval-based baselines, particularly in detecting complex logical inconsistencies. © 2026 IEEE.},
