  abstract = {The integration of Large Language Models (LLMs) into distributed systems promises unprecedented agility in generating algorithms, configurations, and remediation strategies. However, their probabilistic nature introduces fundamental challenges-non-determinism, lack of explicit specifications, and evolving decision logic-that conflict with the strict correctness guarantees required by mission-critical systems. This paper analyzes why state-of-the-art verification frameworks like Anvil (liveness) and IronSync (safety) fail when applied to LLM-driven workflows. We demonstrate how non-deterministic outputs invalidate deterministic verification assumptions, dynamic behavior disrupts static invariants, and missing specifications undermine composable correctness. To address these challenges, we propose a research agenda bridging formal methods and LLM-driven workflows, including modular verification, specification mining, verification-aware training, and adaptive runtime monitoring. Our work provides a roadmap for building verifiable LLM-driven systems, balancing agility with the rigorous assurance required for safety-critical applications.  © 2025 IEEE.},
