  abstract = {The UML Testing Profile (UTP) Version 2.1 (October 2019) provides a standardized extension of the Unified Modeling Language (UML) for specifying test architectures, procedures, and verdicts in model-based testing. UTP plays a central role in aligning test design with system models. In this paper, the benefits and challenges of representing UTP as an ontology are explored. In particular, the ways in which test engineers can leverage semantic web technologies such as reasoning and querying are explored. The approach also highlights potential semantic inconsistencies in the representation of the profile. A subset of UTP is translated into the Ontological Modeling Language (OML), a formal language designed to support precise, semantically rich modeling across systems engineering domains. Using OML, an initial ontological representation of core UTP constructs is developed, including test sets, test cases, test procedures, verdicts, and data. In doing so, UTP is assessed from an ontological perspective, and several structural inconsistencies and modeling ambiguities that complicate its formalization are identified. This work underscores the challenges of aligning semiformal modeling profiles like UTP with ontological foundations. The UTP ontologies developed in this study lay the groundwork for a more principled ontological analysis of test design in Model-Based System Engineering (MBSE). By modeling UTP in OML, this effort contributes to the broader goal of integrating testing more effectively into formal ontological frameworks, enabling traceability, interoperability, and automated analysis across systems and software lifecycle activities. © 2025 IEEE.},
