  abstract = {Compliance verification of autonomous vehicle behavior represents a fundamental requirement for transportation system deployment. While human drivers handle natural language ambiguities intuitively, autonomous vehicles require precise formal specifications to eliminate linguistic uncertainties and ensure compliance. However, global traffic regulations exhibit substantial variations, creating significant challenges for formal specification languages in representing diverse natural language regulations across jurisdictions. Current approaches face critical limitations: manual formalization methods are labor-intensive and lack cross-regional adaptability, while existing Large Language Model (LLM)-based translation methods suffer from limited representability of traffic concept semantics and generate incorrect representations of temporal constraints and logical relationships due to insufficient reasoning accuracy. This paper presents AutoLegal, a novel translation framework leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs) for systematic traffic regulation formalization. The framework integrates semantic foundations with knowledge graphs to provide LLMs with semantic anchors that ensure representability guarantees and atomic predicate consistency. To address logical reasoning demands, the framework incorporates explicit tree-structured logical expressions within Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, transforming implicit reasoning into interpretable and verifiable processes for enhanced temporal-logical relationship handling. A dual verification mechanism combining syntax validation and semantic back-translation validation with iterative refinement ensures final output quality and system robustness. Experimental results validate AutoLegal’s capability for translating traffic regulations into Signal Temporal Logic, achieving a translation success rate of 73.7% that surpasses other LLM-based methods, while the coverage of rule representations exceeds manual translation. Analysis of atomic predicate semantic representation performance and large language model hallucination mitigation capabilities further explains AutoLegal’s superior performance. The generated STL specifications demonstrate verified monitorability and semantic accuracy in real-world scenarios, thereby facilitating the advancement of intelligent transportation systems. © 2000-2011 IEEE.},
