  abstract = {This experimental study investigates the feasibility and effectiveness of automatically translating natural language requirements into ANSI/ISO C Specification Language (ACSL) for formal verification of airborne software using Large Language Models (LLM) combined with Retrieval-Enhanced Generation (RAG) technology. The proposed workflow constructs a loop of model generation, model verification, and model refinement in ACSL. Experimental results show that, compared to manually writing specifications, the LLM+RAG workflow significantly shortens specification generation time, substantially reduces the high error rate caused by using LLM alone, and maintains higher syntactic and functional intent correctness. The study found that the failure modes of the generated specifications mainly involve logical inconsistencies rather than syntactic errors, and the model's self-censorship mechanism effectively mitigates these logical inconsistency problems. The results also indicate that this workflow is most effective for requirements with clear patterns and precedents in the knowledge base, providing a promising automated method for the verification of airborne safety-critical systems.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
